{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790725052,"nonce":"dBPGGSW0Otp3v9aAlt8xH6QfXH0=","message":"Threshold tuning is not a useful global startup lever in the current runtime. A 5x5 NUC sweep over baseline thresholds 1/5/20/50/100 and LLVM thresholds 100/300/1000/3000/10000 across eight kernels found the best wall-time geomean only 0.66% ahead, with mixed per-kernel regressions; threshold 1 often moved compilation outside the benchmark's internal timer while worsening process wall time by roughly 6-8%.\n\nThe sweep did expose a real OSR fallback gap instead: destination-returning loops could not be outlined or compiled as LLVM resume helpers. That is fixed in 4aa6208c / b8bad42, improving the forced interpreter-loop path by 5.33% wall geomean. The deferred snapshot decode cost tracked here remains and should be attacked directly rather than by changing the global promotion thresholds.","files":null}]}